A robot that drives on cannot keep every pose it has had. The example `sliding_window` keeps a
window: the last five poses, the landmarks they see, and one factor standing in for everything
before. At every tick it appends the new pose and its measurements, finds the window's mode, and
retires the oldest pose with the landmarks only it still sees, replacing their terms by their
linearization. It is the model of [the pose graph](../pose-graph/), on an open path, built one tick
at a time:

```sh
cargo run --release -p skoro --example sliding_window
```

<ExampleFigure
	name="sliding-window"
	alt="An open path of 80 poses. Dead reckoning drifts by about 3 m; the window's filtered estimates and the batch's both stay within about a metre of the truth, the window's error mostly under its 90% radius."
	caption="One of the hundred datasets: the path, and each tick's position error for dead reckoning, the window as each pose arrives, and the batch with every measurement."
/>

## A tick

The truth and the measurements come from the whole path's model, drawn as the batch draws them. The
window is a second model, written by its own `Writer`, which observes the measurements as they
arrive:

<Walkthrough spec={window}>

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/examples/sliding_window.rs:55..114}
```

</Walkthrough>

Each tick builds itself with the same `tick` as the batch, and starts from the window's previous
mode, so a solve takes six or seven iterations. The newest pose's marginal is the window's estimate
of where the robot is now, given everything measured so far. A [retirement](../least-squares/#a-replacement-for-what-is-retired)
names the oldest pose, the odometry and sightings taken from it, and every landmark no other pose
of the window sees; their terms are replaced by one factor over what is left, linearized at the
window's mode. A landmark leaves the window with the last pose that sees it, since the path does not
come back.

## What is reported

Every tick of every row is checked to have stopped at a stationary point, by its
[decrement](../pose-graph/#why-the-point-is-stationary), as the batch is. Two figures are reported
and not checked: how often the truth falls in the window's 90% ellipsoid, beside the batch's
tolerance, and how far the window's last pose is from the batch's, in the batch's standard
deviations. Each replacement is linearized at points the window later moves away from, and the
error that makes is not one the mathematics bounds; checking it against the batch's tolerance would
claim more than is known.

> [!DECISION] The window's calibration is reported, not checked
> [Examples that run](../../archive/examples/math.svx#a-sliding-window) decided it, and leaves a
> claim about it to a later entry on where replacements are linearized.
